WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.700
Starlink is beta testing a program allowing property owners to earn passive income by sharing satellite internet with nearby users. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 212. Starlink is launching a beta path for property owners to turn a single kit into a shared access business. Hosts install one dish and router in locations with demand, like apartment complexes or campgrounds. Neighbors by short term passes for hour, day, week, or month.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.060
Starlink handles account creation, payments, and the satellite link.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.560
The program is accepting host applications in multiple countries, earnings tied directly

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.060
to each paid connection, while the host manages placement and basic upkeep.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:49.260
The Starlink Communities program opens new avenues for expanding internet access in remote areas

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.480
while providing income opportunities.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:56.540
Property owners can leverage existing infrastructure without additional hardware costs.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:01.320
This approach mirrors other sharing economy models in space technology.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.460
It could accelerate coverage in underserved regions.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.860
The beta is already accepting applications across multiple countries.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.860
A nearby star system with two suns could potentially support an Earth-sized planet

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.020
in a region where liquid water could exist.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.080
The paper focuses on 70 Ophioki, about 5 Parsecs away.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.820
The two stars are 88% and 73% of the sun's mass.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:31.260
NASA. Researchers from UC Riverside modeled stable orbits in the habitable zone around

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.320
the larger star. The paper is published in the astrophysical journal.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.340
Doctoral student Skyler Diongeolillo conducted the dynamical modeling using computer simulations.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.360
Imagine the distance from Earth to the nearest star system scaled to the length of a football

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:52.320
field. In 70 of Yuki, the two stars orbit each other at separations that still allow

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.340
a planet to circle one without being pulled away. The larger star at 88% the sun's mass

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:03.920
defines a habitable zone where temperatures could allow liquid water. Computer simulations

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.920
tracked an Earth mass planet over long periods and found stable paths inside that zone despite

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:15.880
the gravitational tug from the smaller companion star. The key is the orbital architecture, with

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.260
the secondary star far enough not to destabilize the inner region yet close enough to influence

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:26.700
over all dynamics. One surprising number is the system's proximity at only five

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:32.180
parsecs, making it a prime target for future observations. NASA is providing

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:38.100
Boeing with a new contract and $359 million. The funding aims to return the

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:43.160
starliner to flight and certify the Vulcan rocket. This support comes after previous

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.940
challenges with the spacecraft. New simulations suggest the Milky Way may have

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:53.660
begun as thousands of galaxies. The Megatron project took three years to run on high-powered

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:59.840
supercomputers. Led by Harley Katz at the University of Chicago, the team produced six papers.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:06.000
The papers are published in the Open Journal of Astrophysics. The models show the early region as

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:13.780
a collection of smaller galaxies, some pumping out new stars. On October 3, 1942, the V2 was fired

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:19.540
from Pina Munde, Germany. It was the fourth test and the first to succeed. The rocket rose to

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:26.280
an altitude of 60 miles. The 12-ton rocket carried a one-ton warhead. The launch proved significant

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:32.360
for both military and space exploration. Photographer Huang Dundong captured the Gagan shine,

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:37.660
a faint glow of sunlight scattered back toward Earth by dust grains in the inner solar system.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.360
The image shows the glow in Aquarius at the point directly opposite the Sun.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:48.420
the Milky Way arcs across the center in the all-sky view. Looking back over the past week,

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.920
the biggest running stories have been NASA's steady support for commercial crew vehicles,

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.620
SpaceX's rapid launch cadence, and continued progress on next-generation telescopes.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:05.860
NASA's new $359 million Boeing contract builds directly on earlier Starliner recovery efforts,

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:11.940
While SpaceX completed three Falcon launches and a dragon docking in under 13 hours earlier

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:17.560
in the week, meanwhile, fresh web and ground-based data keep refining our picture of early

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.860
galaxies and Mars' ice composition.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:26.700
A Harvard-led team detected a radio-emission signal from BetaPictorus B more than 63 light

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:27.300
years away.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.480
The signal shows characteristics of a rural activity using the Miracat telescope.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.360
The study appears on the RXIV pre-print server.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:41.680
Creating artificial gravity on a spaceship would require either constant acceleration or

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.220
large rotating structures.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.880
Current engineering limits make both approaches difficult for long duration missions.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.920
The Mars Society published an article on protecting Mars mission electronics from solar radiation.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.280
Written by Lou Ferrell, it discusses strategies for future missions.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:03.260
and article suggests space exploration should learn from deep-sea technology.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:08.820
It was submitted to the space subreddit and discusses potential crossovers in engineering approaches.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.580
A user lost their engineering job due to bad management.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.120
They are shifting to creating astronomy videos from research papers and news.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.760
The channel will include blender animations, video editing, and voiceovers.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.420
They aim for high quality and accuracy.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:30.080
Two friends started a space channel with one doing voiceover in script, the other animations

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:30.840
and research.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:35.540
Their latest video discusses what would really happen if Betelgeuse exploded.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.400
They avoided sensationalism in the content.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:45.420
A user with an MSC in applied physics and PhD in hall thruster simulations asks about jobs

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.660
that involve direct contact with astronauts on the ISS.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.140
They are European and interested in alternatives to becoming an astronaut.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:58.660
not. Researchers link cosmic fingerprints of the universe's first stars with the Megatron

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:04.280
simulation. The work draws on new data from the James Webb Space Telescope to refine early

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:10.240
galaxy models. NASA astronaut Christina Koch will join fans at the Philadelphia Eagles versus

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.680
